  "analysis": "The patient's presentation of progressive decrease in visual acuity and increased sensitivity to light, coupled with the finding of discrete brown deposits on the corneal epithelium, is indicative of a side effect associated with certain antipsychotic medications. Among the options provided, Chlorpromazine is well-documented in medical literature to cause such ocular deposits, specifically in the form of corneal and lens deposits, which can lead to symptoms as described by the patient. This condition is known as chlorpromazine-induced corneal deposits. The other antipsychotics listed (Clozapine, Haloperidol, Thioridazine, and Ziprasidone) are either associated with different ocular effects or do not commonly cause corneal deposits. Thioridazine, while also known to cause retinal deposits leading to pigmentary retinopathy, does not typically result in corneal deposits as described in this case. Therefore, based on the symptoms, clinical findings, and known side effects of the medications listed, Chlorpromazine is the most likely antipsychotic that this patient has been taking, leading to the observed ocular changes.",
